 2.1 Suppose $B>0$ and $J:=\{i:b_i=0<a_i\}$ is nonempty. If $f_\infty=+\infty$, the right side is $+\infty$, so the inequality holds. If any $b_i>0=a_i$ with $f(0)=+\infty$, the right side is $+\infty$ and the inequality holds. Otherwise, if $f_\infty$ is finite, replace each $b_i=0$ with $i\in J$ by $b_i^{(\eta)}=\eta>0$, leaving all other second coordinates unchanged. the finite Jensen inequality [F2] then gives $(B+|J|\eta)f(A/(B+|J|\eta))\le\sum_{b_i>0}b_if(a_i/b_i)+\sum_{i\in J}\eta f(a_i/\eta)$. As $\eta\downarrow0$, the left side tends to $Bf(A/B)$ by [F3] at the positive number $A/B$, and each last summand tends to $a_i f_\infty$ by the definition of $f_\infty$. The resulting inequality is exactly $\Pi_f(A,B)\le\sum_i\Pi_f(a_i,b_i)$. [F1, F2, F3, given, construct, algebra] ∎
